## Answer

The cross product map is an **isomorphism of graded $R$-algebras**:

$$H^{*}(X;R)\otimes_{R}H^{*}(Y;R)\stackrel{\times}{\longrightarrow}H^{*}(X\times Y;R).$$

## Proof

This is the cohomological Künneth theorem. We verify the hypotheses and conclude.

**Hypotheses.** $X,Y$ are CW complexes, $R$ is a commutative ring (with unit, as is standard for the cup/cross product), and $H^{k}(Y;R)$ is a finitely generated free $R$-module for every $k$.

**Step 1 — Reduction to finite CW approximation of $Y$.** Since $H^{k}(Y;R)$ is finitely generated for all $k$, the cellular cochain complex $C^{*}(Y;R)$ is, in each degree, a finitely generated free $R$-module (cellular cochains are free on the $k$-cells). Moreover, because $H^{k}(Y;R)$ vanishes for $k$ large when $Y$ is finite-dimensional, and is finitely generated in every degree, one may replace $Y$ by a CW subcomplex of finite type capturing all cohomology. Concretely, $Y$ is homotopy equivalent to a CW complex $Y'$ of finite type (each skeleton finite); the product $X\times Y$ is homotopy equivalent to $X\times Y'$, and cohomology is homotopy invariant. Thus we may assume $Y$ has finitely many cells in each dimension, so each $C^{k}(Y;R)$ is finitely generated free.

**Step 2 — The Eilenberg–Zilber / Künneth chain-level situation.** The cellular cochain complex of $X\times Y$ is chain-homotopy equivalent to $\operatorname{Hom}(C_{*}(X)\otimes C_{*}(Y),R)$ via the Eilenberg–Zilber theorem. Using the adjunction $\operatorname{Hom}(C_{*}(X)\otimes C_{*}(Y),R)\cong \operatorname{Hom}(C_{*}(X),R)\otimes \operatorname{Hom}(C_{*}(Y),R)$ — which holds because each $C_{k}(Y)$ is free and finitely generated (so the tensor–Hom interchange is exact here) — we obtain a chain homotopy equivalence

$$C^{*}(X\times Y;R)\simeq C^{*}(X;R)\otimes_{R} C^{*}(Y;R).$$

The cross product is induced by this chain-level map.

**Step 3 — Algebraic Künneth theorem (cohomological form).** We now apply the algebraic Künneth theorem to the tensor product of cochain complexes $C^{*}(X;R)\otimes_{R}C^{*}(Y;R)$. The short exact Künneth sequence in cohomology reads

$$0\to \bigoplus_{i+j=k}H^{i}(X;R)\otimes_{R}H^{j}(Y;R)\to H^{k}(X\times Y;R)\to \bigoplus_{i+j=k+1}\operatorname{Tor}_{R}\!\bigl(H^{i}(X;R),H^{j}(Y;R)\bigr)\to 0.$$

The key point: each $H^{j}(Y;R)$ is **free** over $R$ by hypothesis. A free module is flat, so

$$\operatorname{Tor}_{R}\!\bigl(H^{i}(X;R),H^{j}(Y;R)\bigr)=0\quad\text{for all }i,j.$$

Therefore the Tor term vanishes identically, and the Künneth sequence collapses to isomorphisms

$$\bigoplus_{i+j=k}H^{i}(X;R)\otimes_{R}H^{j}(Y;R)\xrightarrow{\;\cong\;}H^{k}(X\times Y;R)$$

for every $k$. The map realizing these isomorphisms is precisely the cross product (it is the map induced on cohomology by the chain-level cross product / Eilenberg–Zilber equivalence).

**Step 4 — Grading and ring structure.** Assembling the degree-$k$ isomorphisms over all $k$ gives an isomorphism of graded $R$-modules

$$H^{*}(X;R)\otimes_{R}H^{*}(Y;R)\cong H^{*}(X\times Y;R).$$

Moreover, the cross product is multiplicative: for $\alpha,\alpha'\in H^{*}(X;R)$ and $\beta,\beta'\in H^{*}(Y;R)$,

$$(\alpha\smile\alpha')\times(\beta\smile\beta')=(-1)^{|\alpha'||\beta|}(\alpha\times\beta)\smile(\alpha'\times\beta'),$$

which is the standard graded-commutativity sign. Under the identification $H^{*}(X\times Y;R)\cong H^{*}(X;R)\otimes H^{*}(Y;R)$, the cup product on $X\times Y$ corresponds to the product on the tensor product of graded $R$-algebras. Hence the cross product is an isomorphism not merely of graded modules but of **graded $R$-algebras**.

**Conclusion.** Under the stated hypotheses, the cross product map

$$H^{*}(X;R)\otimes_{R}H^{*}(Y;R)\stackrel{\times}{\longrightarrow}H^{*}(X\times Y;R)$$

is an **isomorphism of graded $R$-algebras**.

